<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Rome</h1><p class="st-meta">590 CE – 754 CE (approx.) · capital Rome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Byzantine district around Rome in which the popes gradually took over government, the seed of the Papal States.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion, the territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> remained in Byzantine hands, cut off from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a> by Lombard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a>. A duke (dux) commanded its garrison under the exarch of Ravenna. From the pontificate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> (590–604), who organised Rome's food supply and defence, the popes became the area's real leaders.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duke led the local militia while the pope administered the vast Church estates, fed the city and negotiated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a>. A local military aristocracy grew powerful. Disputes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> over doctrine and taxation, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm">Iconoclasm</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-iii-the-isaurian">Leo III</a>, loosened ties to the empire. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liutprand-king-of-the-lombards">Liutprand</a>'s gift of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a> to the pope in 728 was its first territorial grant.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ravenna">Ravenna</a> in 751 and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and gained the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> in 754. Pepin's donation of 756 created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>, into which the duchy was absorbed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-rome.jpg" alt="Duchy of Rome" width="320" height="146"><figcaption>Image: Amonixinator; derivative work by Hoodinski · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ByzantineEmpire717%2Bextrainfo%2Bthemes.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>590 CE – 754 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gregory the Great</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chalcedonian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ducatus Romanus, Byzantine Duchy of Rome</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=672&amp;focus=duchy-of-rome" aria-label="Duchy of Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 686 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Rome lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 942L5292 863M5304 942L5709 970M5304 942L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5292 863h0M5709 970h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rome. <a href="/atlas?year=672&amp;focus=duchy-of-rome">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
